<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolsheviks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bolshevik Party, RSDLP(b), Reds</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bolsheviks, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, were a radical faction of the Marxist Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP) that split with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> at the Second Party Congress in 1903, where Lenin argued for a smaller party of professional revolutionaries. Their name came from the Russian word for majority, as Lenin's faction won the votes on most important issues. The faction became a separate party in 1912, seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 and suppressed opposition during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>; after 1921 it was the sole legal party. Renamed the Russian Communist Party (Bolsheviks) in 1918, it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> in 1952.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolsheviks.jpg" alt="Bolsheviks" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presidium_of_the_9th_Congress_of_the_Russian_Communist_Party_(Bolsheviks).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
